A||N||5||SeanyD||whirling noise in the back left corner||||1||
Z||000000||SeanyD||07-17-2000||10:50 PM||||I have an 84 se and when I drive I get this wierd whirling noise from the back, I had the inner tie rod(?) in that corner go.....an expensive part and it seems to be common because it also happend in my 85, and it's almost like the bearing is shot...any input??  how hard's it to replace the bearing?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Oreif||07-18-2000||03:24 AM||||You cannot replace the bearings, you have to replace the entire hub assembly. Not to bad to do. The hard part is finding a 30mm deepwell socket to remove the center bolt. The new hub should have a new nut for this. The nuts is a clinch type. Once installed and removed it is not recommended to use again. You take off the center nut then there are 3 bolts to remove and the old one is off. Hubs cost $60 to $90 each depending on where you buy them from.||||reg||1
Z||000002||SeanyD||07-18-2000||01:04 PM||||Thanks oreif, I had known that the whole assembly had to go....but is it likely that a bad assembly is what's making the noise??||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||07-18-2000||01:49 PM||||You can raise car and remove tire, caliper and rotor. Then wiggle the hub and see if there is any play in it. If you raise the entire backend you can turn the hub slolwly and listen to it. If you hear and noise that sounds like sand or the hub doesn't turn smoothly. It can be the hub/bearings. Also since everything is out of the way you can look at the CV joint too. If there is grease splattered it means that there is a hole in the CV boot. Once the grease is gone CV joints don't last long. That way you'll be able to determine what exacly is making the noise.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Gold-86SE||07-18-2000||02:17 PM||||<BR>Before you remove the wheel and start disassembling the back, raise the rear and test the hub in the following fashion.<P>Grab the tire with one hand in the 12  o'clock position and the other in the 6 o'clock position, push and pull on the wheel (rocking it from top to bottom). Does it move?<P>Now, grab the tire with one hand in the 9  o'clock position and the other in the 3 o'clock position, push and pull on the wheel (from left to right). Does it move?<P>If there is any movement the wheel hub needs replacement and it is as easy as oreif says.<P><P>------------------<BR>Gold-86SE (Fiero Collector)||||reg||1
Z||000005||SeanyD||07-18-2000||02:26 PM||||thanks guys, I';ll have a look at the before I drop the motor...the car is schedualed for a new clutch!||||reg||1
